    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters August 17 with a high-ceiling lineup led by **Corbin Carroll** (68.93) and **Austin Riley** (68.60). **Corbin Carroll** remains our premier offensive engine against Gamboa; despite a slight platoon penalty for the lefty-lefty matchup, the favorable park factors and his elite slot in the #2 hole elevate his profile significantly. **Austin Riley** stands out as our most intriguing value play today. While the #8 spot in the lineup typically dampens expectations, his matchup against a high-xERA arm in Ober\u2014combined with favorable wind conditions and a slow delivery from the mound that invites stolen base opportunities\u2014pushes his efficiency score well into the \"Strong\" tier.\n\n**Seiya Suzuki** (62.21) also provides excellent production in the #2 spot. While he faces the challenge of a high-percentage hold pitcher in Castillo, his BvP and park-adjusted projection suggest he remains a locked-in starter. **Gabriel Moreno** (57.83) and **Max Muncy** (54.95) occupy the \"Marginal\" territory, serving as functional pieces in the lineup. **Zach McKinstry** (49.68) remains the most volatile start of the group; he faces a sub-optimal platoon situation against Mlodzinski and a catcher with a quick pop time, capping his upside despite his versatility. We have no bench players viable today, as **Jose Caballero** and **Josh Naylor** are facing team off-days, **James Wood** remains sidelined on the IL, and **Mickey Moniak** has not been penciled into the starting nine. We are currently holding **Trevor Story**, **Brice Turang**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Edwin Arroyo**, **Cole Young**, and **Henry Bolte** in reserve, monitoring their availability for upcoming series to preserve our limited game caps. \n\nMonitor the final lineups closely for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Max Muncy**, and **Seiya Suzuki**; all three rely on assumed batting order placements. Any defensive adjustments or late-inning lineup shifts could compress their efficiency scores below our start threshold, particularly for **Gabriel Moreno** and **Max Muncy**, who sit on the lower end of our active rotation rankings.",
